.\" Man page for snifd, the SNIF relay / connector daemon.
.\" Keep in sync with the snif_help string in snifd/help.c and the option
.\" parser in snifd/snifd.c.  Version: SNIF_VERSION_STR.
.TH SNIFD 8 "2026-07-05" "snif 1.03" "SNIF Manual"
.SH NAME
snifd \- SNIF relay and connector daemon (end\-to\-end TLS SNI forwarding)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B snifd
.RB [ \-l
.RI [ bind :] snif_port ]
.RB [ \-s
.RI [ bind :] relay_port ]
.RB [ \-p
.IR push_fifo ]
.RB [ \-i
.IR in_fifo ]
.RB [ \-t
.IR abuse_sense ]
.RB [ \-m
.IR sni_host : fwd_port : dst_host : dst_port ]
.RI [ bind :] fwd_port
.RI [[ bind :] fwd_port " ...]"
.br
.B snifd \-c
.I cert_file
.B \-k
.I pkey_file
.B \-a
.I init_url
.RB [ \-K
.IR key_algo ]
.br
.B snifd
.RB [ \-d ]
.B \-c
.I cert_file
.B \-k
.I pkey_file
.RB [ \-r
.IR snif_host [: relay_port ]]
.RI [ fwd_port :[ dst_host :]][+] dst_tls_port
.RI "| [" fwd_port :[ dst_host :]]^[+] dst_plain_port " ..."
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B snifd
is the core SNIF daemon. SNIF gives a device behind NAT a publicly trusted
HTTPS hostname without exposing its private key: TLS terminates on the device,
and a public relay forwards the still\-encrypted stream by its TLS SNI record.
.B snifd
runs in one of two roles.
.TP
.B Relay
Accepts connections from SNIF connectors on the SNIF port
.RB ( \-l ),
accepts incoming TLS clients on the relay port
.RB ( \-s ),
and forwards each still\-encrypted stream to the connector that registered the
matching SNI name. The relay never holds a key and never terminates TLS, so it
cannot read or alter forwarded traffic.
.TP
.B Connector
Runs on the device. It first initializes a certificate \(em allocating a
permanent SNIF hostname \(em against a CA proxy
.RB ( \-a ),
then connects outbound to a relay
.RB ( \-r )
and forwards accepted streams to local or backend services. A device never
re\-keys: a new key gets a new hostname.
When the relay connection ends or its TLS handshake fails, the connector
reconnects: at once if the relay had answered on that connection, otherwise
after 15 seconds, doubling with each further connection the relay ends
without answering, up to 5 minutes.
.PP
Invoked with invalid arguments,
.B snifd
prints a usage summary. The
.BR snif\-conn (1)
wrapper drives the connector role from
.IR /etc/snif/snif.conf .
.SH OPTIONS
.SS Relay options
.TP
.BI \-l " [bind:]snif_port"
Listen for SNIF connectors on
.IR snif_port .
.TP
.BI \-s " [bind:]relay_port"
Listen for incoming relayed TLS clients on
.IR relay_port .
.TP
.BI \-t " abuse_sense"
Abuse\-detection sensitivity threshold.
.TP
.BI \-m " sni_host:fwd_port:dst_host:[+]dst_port"
Static SNI map entry (repeatable). A TLS client accepted on
.I fwd_port
whose SNI exactly matches
.I sni_host
is forwarded, still encrypted, to
.IR dst_host : dst_port
instead of a SNIF connector. The destination must terminate TLS with a
certificate valid for
.IR sni_host .
A connection with a mapped SNI hostname arriving on a port not mapped for
that hostname is dropped immediately and is never offered to connectors.
Lets the relay host serve its own content without an extra relay leg.
A
.B +
prefix on
.I dst_port
prepends a PROXY protocol v1 header (see
.BR "PROXY PROTOCOL" )
carrying the client's address to the forwarded stream; the addresses are
taken first\-hand from the accepted client socket.
Mirrors
.I SNIF_RELAY_MAP
in
.IR snif.conf .
.SS Connector options
.TP
.BI \-c " cert_file"
Certificate file (PEM). Required for the connector role. At start the chain in
it is verified against the trusted roots (see
.BR ENVIRONMENT ),
the private key and its name, as a downloaded one is. A chain that fails is not
used, and a new certificate is requested from the CA proxy.
.TP
.BI \-k " pkey_file"
Private key file for the certificate.
.TP
.BI \-a " init_url"
CA\-proxy initialization URL; allocates or renews the certificate and its SNIF
hostname (for example
.IR https://snif.snif.xyz:4443/ ).
.TP
.BI \-u " api_url"
Certificate API URL. Defaults to
.IR http://{base_hostname}/snif\-cert/ .
.TP
.BI \-K " key_algo"
Key algorithm for a newly generated key:
.IR RSA [: bits ],
.IR EC [: curve ],
.IR ED25519 ", " ED448 .
Defaults to RSA, or as steered by the CA proxy
.RI ( X\-SNIF\-KeyAlgo ).
.TP
.BI \-r " snif_host[:relay_port]"
Relay host to connect to;
.I relay_port
defaults to the SNIF default port.
.TP
.B \-d
Daemon mode: keep running and forwarding. May be combined with certificate
initialization.
.TP
.B \-P
Generate a portable pseudo\-random hostname (recommended for new instances).
Without it, a legacy platform\-dependent generator is used.
.SS Common options
.TP
.BI \-p " push_fifo"
Path to a FIFO for push notifications.
.TP
.BI \-i " in_fifo"
Path to a control/input FIFO.
.SH PORT MAPPING
Positional arguments describe how traffic is forwarded. In relay mode they are
the ports the relay listens on for incoming TLS clients:
.IR [bind:]fwd_port " ..."
.PP
In connector mode each entry maps a relay\-side port to a local or backend
service (mirroring
.I SNIF_CONN_PORTS
in
.IR snif.conf ):
.TP
.I fwd_port:dst_tls_port
Forward to a TLS backend on localhost (the backend terminates TLS with the SNIF
certificate).
.TP
.I fwd_port:dst_host:dst_tls_port
Forward to a TLS backend on
.IR dst_host .
.TP
.I fwd_port:^dst_plain_port
The connector terminates TLS and forwards plaintext to a local port.
.TP
.I fwd_port:dst_host:^dst_plain_port
Terminate TLS and forward plaintext to
.IR dst_host .
.TP
.I fwd_test_port:^
Serve the built\-in HTTPS test responder.
.PP
A
.B +
prefix on the destination port (combining with
.BR ^ ,
e.g.
.IR 443:+8443 " or " 8080:^+80 )
prepends a PROXY protocol v1 header to the forwarded stream; see
.BR "PROXY PROTOCOL" .
.SH PROXY PROTOCOL
Without it, the destination service sees every relayed connection as
originating from the forwarding
.B snifd
(typically localhost). A
.B +
destination\-port prefix makes
.B snifd
send a HAProxy PROXY protocol v1 line
.RI ( "PROXY TCP4|TCP6 src_addr dst_addr src_port dst_port" )
ahead of the forwarded bytes, so the destination learns the original client
address. The destination listener must be configured to expect the header
(nginx
.IR proxy_protocol ,
Apache
.IR mod_remoteip ,
Postfix, Dovecot, HAProxy, ...); a listener expecting the header rejects
plain connections, so use a dedicated, loopback\-bound or firewalled port.
In connector mode the client address is as reported by the relay and is
only as trustworthy as the relay; suitable for logging, rate limiting or
geo lookups rather than as a sole access\-control input. On a relay static
map the addresses are taken first\-hand from the accepted client socket.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.BR SSL_CERT_FILE ", " SSL_CERT_DIR
The trusted roots: OpenSSL's default paths, which these override. A connector
verifies its certificate chain against them, from
.I cert_file
at start and on each download; a relay verifies the connectors' certificates
against them. For a base domain that issues from a private root, a local CA,
put the root in a file named by
.IR SSL_CERT_FILE .
With only
.I SSL_CERT_FILE
set, the default directory is still searched, so public roots still verify.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /etc/snif/snif.conf
Connector and relay configuration (read by
.BR snif\-conn (1)).
.TP
.I /etc/snif/snif.crt\fR, \fI/etc/snif/snif.key
Default connector certificate and private key.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR snif\-conn (1),
.BR snifdog (8)
.PP
SNIF home page and live demo:
.IR https://snif.host
